To 'flush metrics as fast as possible', a developer adds this to the order API:

function drain() {
  if (buffer.length > 0) metricsBatch.push(buffer.shift());
  process.nextTick(drain);
}
drain();

Each call does almost no work. What happens to incoming HTTP requests on this instance?

A refund job runs:

const receipts = await Promise.all(orders.map((o) => provider.refund(o)));

There are 500 orders. One refund rejects after 50 ms; the other 499 each take about 2 seconds. Which statements are true? Select all that apply.
